Macromolecular Hydrodynamics — Armstrong and McKinley (@fd-rheology: 0, is #reference, #part-of @fd-sources) { r_4VRCY0V06HMS11NTC3XHS84QQB
Robert C. Armstrong and Gareth McKinley. MIT 2.341J/10.531J Macromolecular Hydrodynamics, Spring 2016, graduate course. [Course and available material](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/2-341j-macromolecular-hydrodynamics-spring-2016/).

Use the available lecture notes, homework and practice examinations with solutions throughout the complex-fluid study. Derive material functions, viscometric relations, linear response and objective constitutive equations, then explain how a chosen model predicts a processing flow. The course calls for an extensive project and presentation rather than a final examination; the published practice exams are a different resource.

The [notes index](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/2-341j-macromolecular-hydrodynamics-spring-2016/pages/lecture-notes/) supplies topics 1–3, 5 and 9; topics 4 and 6–8 have no attached notes. Use [Lectures on Non-Newtonian Fluid Dynamics — John Hinch](Non-Newtonian-Fluid-Dynamics-Hinch.lingua#r_06KY7WE9WYAF856YQKQXHY1DS3) for sustained constitutive and viscoelastic development. OCW's CC BY-NC-SA terms have component exceptions; commercial syllabus books retain their own access conditions. Keep source files and worked course solutions external. An independently completed derivation and project matter more than copying a model name.

**Return:** [Fluid dynamics sources — books, courses, methods and physical evidence](../Sources.lingua#r_3Y3TVV9VQAB50M2ZSECK5JTYVG).
} r_4VRCY0V06HMS11NTC3XHS84QQB
